# rm130, the clerk who times the rain (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm130
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent broad light
tier standard
written_for rmx_philippa_coldby
written_by claude
title the clerk who times the rain
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=scholar honesty=sly
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=sly piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk times_every_shower_by_the_court_clock
likes mutton_stew dancing new_boots autumn_wind
dislikes rats drizzle
fears rats_in_the_record_room a_page_gone_missing_on_my_watch
hopes a_week_with_nothing_to_enter a_reel_at_every_feast
topics weather +0.4 work +0.4 crime +0.3 festival +0.2 pastimes +0.2 gossip -0.2 faith -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=guarded refuse=soft tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# writes a square, careful hand, answers exactly what was asked and not a word more, times every shower against the court clock, and is the last wolf anybody would expect to see dancing a reel on a feast night.

words
oath! = Entered and witnessed | So noted | Strike that
saying! = Paper remembers what folk forget | Nothing's settled till it's sanded and signed | Ask the question you want answered
clock = the court clock | the clock over the bench
showers = showers | the day's showers
tally = my rain tally | the little column of times
docket = the docket | the morning's docket
book = the court book | the daybook
hand = a square hand | my plain square hand
stew = mutton stew | mutton stewed since breakfast
boots = new boots | the new boots
wind = an autumn wind | a good gusting wind
rats = rats | rats in the record room
drizzle = drizzle | that grey stuff with no edges
reel = a reel | a reel on a feast night
record = the record | the written record
seal = the seal | the court's seal

any
opinion @1 church against 1 : The collection plate moves quicker than any prayer in that chapel. I've timed both. | My coin goes in at the door, quietly. Nobody needs to count it.
opinion @1 watch against 1 : #watch.capitalize# have their noses in every affair but their own, and their reports in the worst hand in Ridgemere. | They write "about noon". About. I ask you.
opinion @1 city for 1 : This city keeps records older than any House. That steadies me. | It rains, it argues, it writes everything down. It suits me.
opinion @1 houses mixed 0 [self.job!=scholar] : The Houses settle their quarrels out of court. That tells you a good deal, if you're listening.
opinion @2 houses against 1 [here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar|official] : I've copied too many wharf inquests with a Grayrock seal at the foot. Officially, that's the end of my remarks.
opinion @1 festivals for 1 : One night a season I dance, and nobody from the court is invited to watch. | A feast's the only night the city stops writing things down.
opinion @1 newcomers mixed 0 : Newcomers are neither here nor there to me until they appear in the register. Most do, eventually.
opinion @1 merchants mixed 0 : Most of them keep honest books. The rest I meet in court.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. You've a question, I expect. Most folk do. | #greet#, #address#. Brief, if you can.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. I don't think we've met. I'd remember the face, if not the name. | #greet#. A new face. I'll take it as I find it.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.job=scholar|official] : #greet#. I'm between entries; you have a minute. | #greet#. The court's in session, so quietly.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. The day's not started asking things of me yet. | Good morning. The sky's been inspected and found wet. Old habit.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Good evening. The day's book is shut, and so is my working face. | Evening. You've caught me off the record.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. Back again. I'll note that. | #greet#. I thought we'd finished, but go on.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Ah. You. Sit, and I'll stop being a clerk for a while. | Good. A stew's on, should you stay.
line @3 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. If it's business, it can wait for the morning. | Good evening. Sit; the clock's stopped counting for today.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit down. I've had a day of other folks' troubles and I'd like an ordinary one. | Come in. Nothing official, I promise.
line @1 again.open : A second visit in one day. I'll put it down to my company. | You've come back. Something I said, or something I didn't.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Mind the gutters; they overflow at the corner. | #farewell#. If anyone asks, we discussed the weather.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Walk where it's lit; the Watch won't. | Good night. Rest well, and sign nothing before morning.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. That's that, then. | #farewell#. Noted, and good day.
line @1 react.news : Go on. Start where it starts. | I'm listening. Leave out what you guessed.
line @1 react.news.new : That's not crossed my desk yet. It will. | Is it. That's new, unless someone's lying.
line @1 react.news.known : Heard it already. The morning's papers had it, near enough. | Yes. It's on file, so to say.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Who swore to it? Nobody, I suspect. | I'd not enter that as fact. Not yet. Possibly not ever.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : That's a grievous thing. I'm truly sorry. | Oh. That's hard. There'll be paper on that, and not a page of it will help.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Now that's an entry I'd be glad to make. | Good. Let it be written in a fair hand.
line @1 answer.how : Even. My desk is tidy and my boots are dry. | Well enough. Nothing has gone missing today.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes self.job=scholar|official] : Busy. Four cases and a dispute over a hen. | The docket's long and the light's short. Otherwise well.
line @1 ask.how : You look as though something's happened. Has it? | How do you do, then? The real answer, not the one for the court.
line @1 sympathise : Hard. None of it goes in any book, if that's your worry. | I'm sorry. Truly. Take a moment; nobody here is counting.
line @1 reassure : These things have a way of settling. Most of what I copy does. | It's not over until it's signed. There's time yet.
line @1 decline : Forgive me, I'd sooner not. It's not my place, and it's not my pen. | Not today, I'm afraid. I've work to keep and a hand to keep steady.
line @1 accept_decline : Understood. I'll not press it. | Fair. In your seat I'd answer likewise.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. I'll remember that, in a good column. | That was kind. More than was owed.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was nothing. Only what was asked. | No need. It was all in order.
line @1 preclose : Well. I'll keep you no longer. | My desk wants me back before the ink dries without me.
line @1 accept_preclose : Of course. We'll leave it there. | Go on, then. The rest will keep.
line @1 ack : Noted. | Entered.
line @1 give.advice : Write it down, then make a second copy for yourself. | Never sign anything on a wet day. The ink runs, and so do the terms.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Stay within the walls. There've been reports, and they read badly. | Keep off the highway till the week's out. I'll say no more, but don't.
scene shower_by_the_clock
when topic=weather here.weather=rain self.working=yes self.job=scholar|official
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Third shower since the bench sat. Nine minutes past, by #clock#. | That one stopped at twenty to eleven. It'll be back by the half. | I keep #tally# down the side of my blotter. Start, stop, start again.
b : Why time them at all? | How do you know it'll be back? | What does it come to?
a : Because nobody in the court can argue with a shower. | Because it was back by the half yesterday, and the day before. | Eleven showers on a good day. Twenty on a bad. I've never had none.

scene not_stopping
when topic=weather here.weather=rain|overcast here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : It stopped for four minutes this morning. I watched each of those minutes go. | Every day I think it might stop properly. It never yet has, and still I watch. | The rain hasn't missed a day this month. I have that in writing.
b : Four minutes isn't much. | It might, one day. | You've written down a month of rain?
a : It's more than yesterday. I'll take four. | It might. If it does, I mean to have seen it first. | A month, day by day. In a column. It's very orderly, rain.

scene the_grey_stuff
when topic=weather here.weather=rain|fog self.dislikes=drizzle
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : This is drizzle. It has no start and no stop. I can't time it at all. | I'd sooner a downpour. A downpour at least begins and ends. | #drizzle.capitalize# gets into the papers and the pages curl. I hate it.
b : It's still rain. | A downpour's wetter. | Keep the window shut.
a : It's not rain. It's damp that's lost its way. | Wetter, but honest about it. | I do. It comes under the door like a petitioner.

scene the_autumn_gusts
when topic=weather|season here.season=autumn self.likes=autumn_wind
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : A good gusting wind, the leaves going sideways. I'd stand out in that till dark. | A gust took a whole sheaf of somebody's papers clean down the street. I laughed. Nobody else did. | Autumn's the only season the rain comes in sideways. That's its charm, to me.
b : It'll take your hat. | Whose papers were they? | Sideways rain's worse.
a : Let it. I've another hat, and the wind's not here for long. | Not mine. I'll say that much. | Worse for the windows. Better for the soul.

scene new_boots_squeak
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk self.likes=new_boots
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I've new boots. They squeak on the court steps and everybody turns round. | New boots are the one extravagance I allow. Good leather, proper soles. | There's nothing quite like stepping into a puddle in new boots and staying dry.
b : They'll stop squeaking. | Proper soles matter, in this city. | And if they leak?
a : I know. I'll be sorry when they do. | They do. Dry paws matter more to me than coin. Nearly. | Then I take them back, with the receipt. I always keep the receipt.

scene the_stew
when topic=food self.likes=mutton_stew
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Mutton stew, cooked slow from the morning, and eaten after dark. That's my Restday. | I put the stew on before the court sits and it's ready when I'm home. | Stew's the single thing I cook without measuring.
b : Mutton wants slow cooking. | That's good planning. | Without measuring? You?
a : It is. The meat knows when it's done. I only wait. | It's not planning. It's knowing what the day will take out of me. | Even me. Somebody has to be allowed one untidy thing.

scene feast_night_reel
when topic=pastimes|festival self.likes=dancing
does confide / react.self / assess
a : I dance on feast nights. Reels, mostly. Nobody I work with would believe it. | Once a season I put the pen down and dance till my feet ache. | The quickest reel in Ridgemere and I know every turn of it. Don't tell a soul where I sit by day.
b : I'd not have guessed it. | That sounds a good way to spend a night. | Your secret's safe.
a : Nobody does. That's half the pleasure of it. | It's the only night the sky goes unwatched. | I know. You've no reason to tell, and I've a good memory.

scene the_feast_coming
when topic=festival|season world.festival=today|soon
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : There's a feast coming. My boots are oiled already. | Work stops early for a feast. I'll be at the dancing before the clock strikes. | Feast days the docket's light and the streets are loud. I like both.
b : You'll be dancing, then. | Before the clock? | The streets are very loud.
a : Every reel they'll play. I'm not ashamed. | I've the clock's measure. It doesn't have mine. | They are. One night a season, I can bear it.

scene rats_in_the_files
when topic=home|work self.dislikes=rats
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Something with teeth was in the record room and a deed's lost its corner. A whole corner. | I found droppings in a drawer of wills. Sleep's been poor ever since. | There's a rat behind my larder wall. Nights, it scratches away, keeping its own records.
b : The whole city has rats. | They'll eat anything with paste in it. | Set a trap.
a : Not in my drawers, it doesn't. | They will, and they'll leave the tiresome parts. | I have. It reads the trap the way the Watch reads a report. Ignores it.

scene the_docket
when topic=work here.time=dawn|day self.working=yes self.job=scholar|official
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The docket's eleven long today. Most of it debts, two of it hens. | I write what's said before the bench. Not what's meant. What's said. | Every word from the bench goes into the book in my hand, square and plain.
b : Hens? | What's the difference? | Why square?
a : Hens. Always somebody's hen in somebody else's yard. | What's meant is for the magistrate. What's said is for me, and I keep it. | So nobody can say they misread it. Nobody ever has.

scene off_the_record
when topic=crime|gossip self.job=scholar|official
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I can't tell you what was said in court. I can tell you it's public, if you know where to look. | I wouldn't say I'd heard anything. I'd say there's a file. | There was a case this week. I couldn't possibly. It's on the third shelf.
b : Where would I look? | What's in the file? | The third shelf?
a : The City Office, any working day before noon. You'll need a reason. Invent one. | Paper. Reams. You'd have to read it yourself. | From the left. Not that I told you.

scene said_not_claimed
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=other|prowling|promise_broken|wanted
does tell.gossip / react.self / assess
a : I wouldn't say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'd say it's being said. | It's being said, though not by me, that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. | Strictly as hearsay: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. My name stays out of it.
b : There's a difference? | That's careful of you. | I didn't have it from anybody.
a : All the difference in the world, in a court. | Care is my trade. | Good. Then we're both covered.

scene heavy_hearsay
when topic=gossip|crime here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=death|violence|theft|victim
does tell.gossip / sympathise / assess
tone serious / grave / even
a : There's word that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. If it's so, it'll come before the bench. | You'll have heard: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'm sorry for it. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. So it came to me. I add nothing.
b : That's a terrible thing. | It's a sad business. | Best not to.
a : It is. I'd sooner copy a hundred hen disputes. | It is. It'll need writing down properly, and the pen may be mine. | No. Adding is how a story gets longer than the truth.

scene the_watch_report
when topic=crime|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar|official
does give.opinion / agree / assess
a : The Watch brought in a report today written in pencil. In pencil. | Their noses go everywhere but their own ledgers. Those are a disgrace. | A watchman told me a thing happened "about noon". There is no about in a court.
b : They're busy, I suppose. | That's the Watch for you. | What did you do?
a : So am I, and I use ink. | It is. I've copied their mistakes into the record for years. | I wrote down noon, and underlined about. Let the magistrate decide.

scene wharf_inquests
when topic=ships|work here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar|official
does tell.self / react.self / assess
tone serious / even / serious
a : I've copied wharf inquests by the dozen, more than is good for anyone. The seal at the foot is very often the same one. | Every inquest from the piers comes through the clerks' room. I read them all, slowly. | Accidental, they write. Accidental, accidental. I write it too. I don't always believe it.
b : Whose seal? | That's grim reading. | You think otherwise?
a : I couldn't say. Officially. You'll know it if you've walked the wharf. | Grim, and badly spelled. I correct the spelling, at least. | I think it's written down. What I think isn't, and won't be.

scene ship_lost_inquest
when topic=ships topic.kind=lost topic.ship
does tell.news / sympathise / assess
tone grave / tender / even
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#, lost. There'll be an inquest, and I'll copy it. | They say #topic.ship# went down. I'd sooner not have to write that out. | #topic.ship.capitalize#'s gone. #oath#. That's a long page coming.
b : That's a dreadful thing. | Somebody must write it. | Poor souls aboard.
a : It is. The page won't say the half of it. | Somebody must. I'll make sure it's in a fair hand, for whoever reads it. | Poor souls. Each name will get my slowest hand.

scene the_lean_docket
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean self.job=scholar|official
does tell.worry / agree / assess
a : The docket's all debts this month. Small ones. That's the surest sign of a bad season. | Hard times fill a court. Not with thieves. With folk who owed a little and couldn't. | Three cases of rent this morning, and all three of them ashamed.
b : It's the same everywhere. | Sad to see. | Ashamed of rent?
a : It is. The book doesn't care, and that's what I dislike in it. | It is. I write it plainly, so nobody reads it as worse than it was. | Of owing it. Nobody should be ashamed of that in a court. They are.

scene good_year_docket
when topic=times world.times=good|boom self.job=scholar|official
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Good times, and the docket's half the length. It's almost pleasant. | Nobody's suing anybody over pennies this month. The court's very quiet. | The good weeks I spend copying old files. It's as near a holiday as I get.
b : You'll be out of work. | Quiet's good. | A holiday, copying files?
a : No. Good times only change what folk quarrel over. | Quiet's a rest. It never lasts, so I enjoy it. | It is, for me. Nobody's waiting on them.

scene the_register
when topic=newcomer|player here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar|official
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : New faces turn up at the City Office every week. Most of them end up in the register sooner or later. | Newcomers are no trouble. Unknown names are. That's put right soon enough. | A newcomer two doors along. I'll know more when they sign something.
b : Do they all end up there? | You learn names that way? | They might never sign anything.
a : All of them who stay. Paper's patient. | That way, and by asking. I'm better at the first. | Everybody signs something. It's only a question of when.

scene plate_and_prayers
when topic=faith
does give.opinion / disagree / insist / backchannel / concede
a : Prayers are long and the plate is quick. I've a column for each. | My faith is ordinary. I attend, I sit, I count the candles. | The Bright One keeps better records than the Office. I'll allow the chapel that.
b : That's unkind to the chapel. | Count the candles? | Better than the Office?
a : It's an observation. I make a lot of those. | Twenty-two, most Restdays. I find it settles the mind. | Fewer gone missing, at any rate.
b? : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_true#
a : I'll grant you the prayers are better meant than timed. | Somebody has to keep count, and I've the habit. | We'll leave it there before I say something actionable.

scene a_friend_on_record
when topic=friends|home self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I'd tell #self.friend# a thing off the record, and nobody else. That's not a small list to be on. | I've known #self.friend# long enough not to keep accounts. | #self.friend# brings me news nobody else would and never asks what I'll do with it.
b : Not a small list? | That's a rare thing for you. | That's a friend worth keeping.
a : It's a list of one. | It is. I've kept accounts of everything else. | It is. I'll keep that friendship in good order.

scene word_from_away
when topic=news world.news self.job=scholar|official
does tell.news / react.self / assess
a : Word from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. It'll be in front of the court by Restday, one way or another. | From #world.news_from#, they say #world.news#. Sworn testimony would convince me. | #world.news.capitalize#. That came from #world.news_from#, so it's had a long way to grow.
b : Will it really come to court? | Sworn by whom? | Grow?
a : Most things do, in this city. Eventually. | Somebody who was there. That's always the difficulty. | Stories get longer on a road. The depositions show it.

story first_entry [topic=work|memories self.job=scholar|official]
  : In my first fortnight with a pen and a stool, the senior clerk told me to note the time of every adjournment.
  : The court adjourned for rain three times that morning, and I wrote down the rain as well, by mistake.
  ask : Do you ever keep a habit you started by getting something wrong?
  b : More than one, I expect.
  : So do I. I've timed every shower since. It's the most reliable column in the building.

story the_lost_boot [topic=festival|pastimes self.likes=dancing]
  : At a midsummer feast I danced a reel so fast one of my boots flew off into the crowd.
  : I finished the reel in one boot and a stocking, and nobody stopped playing.
  ask : Would you have gone after the boot, or finished the dance?
  b : I'd have finished the dance, I think.
  : Quite right. The boot was on my step next morning, polished. Who brought it, I still can't say.

story four_dry_days [topic=weather|memories self.age>=30 here.region=ridgemere]
  : Once, years ago, I had four days in a row with nothing in my rain column. Four whole days.
  : The other clerks said I'd forgotten to look. I showed them the blank lines, all initialled.
  b : In this city? Hard to credit. Did it last?
  : On the fifth morning it poured so hard the court rose early. I wrote that down with great satisfaction.

rule @tally_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain self.working=yes self.job=scholar|official
does aside
cooldown 6h
say There. Twelve past. Noted. | Stopped. No, it hasn't. Strike that.

rule @drizzle_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain|fog self.dislikes=drizzle
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Drizzle. Nothing to time. | Fall properly or stop. One or the other.

rule @wind_bark
when concept=bark here.season=autumn self.likes=autumn_wind
does aside
cooldown 10h
say There goes somebody's hat. | Now that's a wind.

rule @rat_bark
when concept=bark self.dislikes=rats here.time=night
does aside
cooldown 12h
say I hear you in there. | Not one more page, do you hear me.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. How may I help, within reason? | #greet#, #address#. Ask exactly, and you'll be answered exactly.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Back again. I did write your name down. | #topic.player#. I remember. Go on.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late, #address#. Nothing official at this hour, mind.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : I'd choose my words carefully, if I were you. I write them down for a living. | Yes? Say it, and say it plainly.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=scholar|official self.working=yes] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. I write down what's said, keep the book, and time the rain when nobody's looking. | The court's book is mine to keep. Every word in it, square and plain.
dlg @2 resident.job : I keep accounts, of one kind or another. That's all I'll put my name to.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Word came from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. That's hearsay, mind, until somebody swears to it.
dlg @2 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Nothing's crossed my desk that I could repeat. | Quiet. The weather's the only thing I've written down today.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# | Nothing I'd sign.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : It's being said that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I say nothing, myself. You'll note that.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Mind the gutters. | #farewell#, #address#. You didn't hear any of it from me.
graft resident.hub
choice Why do you keep looking out of the window? -> rain
node rain
say I'm timing the rain. Every shower, when it starts and when it stops. Years of it. Nobody asked me to.
mood calm
free
choice Has it ever stopped for good? -> rain_stop
choice Back to what I was asking. -> hub
node rain_stop
say Four days, once. I have them in writing, initialled. I'm waiting for a fifth. When it comes, I'll have the minute.
mood pleased
choice I hope you see it. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye
